From 677160b76d2977e6b99dfcf61896922dfbc38fd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vincent <vincent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 07:26:37 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a Codex turn compacted mid-flight keeps the rule it
 began with (#3434)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A group's rules decide as a user's turn begins and hold for the rest of it.
They were held by a key of the group, the conversation and the conversation's
first words (firstWords), and by the parsed turn count: a request arriving
mid-turn stays with what it decided only while that count still reads the
same. Codex's compaction replaces a conversation's first user message with a
summary and renumbers the transcript, so the same native turn arrived under a
new key, read as a fresh turn, and the classifier was asked again on the
summary — a call of magpie's own, and a route decided by the summary rather
than by the task (e2720515-cloud: the parsed route turn went 7 to 1 right
after a compaction, with a fresh classification for the same turn).

Codex names the turn it is in: client_metadata's x-codex-turn-metadata,
turn_id, read by nativeTurn (which requestSessionMetadata already parsed). A
plain turn now holds its decision by that turn — the key's task is a digest of
it, and an entry remembers the turn it was decided for — so a compaction's
rewritten first words and renumbered transcript do not decide it again. A new
native turn is a new task, classified as before.

A helper's call is left as it was: a title, a description, a prediction, or a
subagent's own task arrives with a kind (callKind, x-openai-subagent among
them), so nativeTurn names no turn for it and its own first words keep telling
it apart — a delegation inside a turn still establishes its own task. An agent
that names no turn (Claude Code, ZCode, DSH, a Chat client) keeps the first
words' key exactly as before. Expiry (stickKeep), rule applicability, the
effort picks and the capacity checks are untouched, and the nested groups'
keys inherit the turn from the group's.

RED, on the clean base with the test kept: through the real handler, a ruled
group with an intent classifier and Codex's own metadata —
`go test ./internal/gateway -run TestCodexCompactionKeepsTheTurnsRule -count=1`
fails in 0.03s: "after compaction: &{N:1 Use:b/big ... Held:false ...
Classified:0x...}, classifier asked 2 times". GREEN with the fix: ok in 0.03s,
the same turn held with no second call; a new native turn and a subagent's
task are each classified once more, as they must be.

Ran: the rule and classifier tests (TestRule*, TestIntent*, TestClassif*,
TestDecide*, TestNested*, TestPause*, and the new one) — 65 pass.
`go test ./internal/gateway -count=1` ok except TestClaudeClientCacheAbsentUnchanged,
red on the clean base too (a real `claude` binary's args in this environment,
unrelated). `gofmt -l internal/gateway` clean. `go vet ./...`, `GOOS=linux
GOARCH=amd64 go vet -tags nogui ./...`, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet
./...`, `go build ./...`, `GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -tags nogui ./...`
and `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./...` all exit 0.

Not verified: a real Codex compaction — the test is Codex's protocol shape
(client_metadata, turn_id) through the real handler, not a captured
production request, and no real classifier or model was asked.

Fixes #3434
